Note: So, As we know that, there are two important types of data involved in a VM: which is VM host data & VM guest data.

Let's go through them one by one.

> So, VM host data- This data relates to the Hyper-V session managing the guest operating systems, and includes information about CPU, network, and disk utilization
 Meaning that, VM host data comes from Hyper-v where our VM is deployed.

Note: ofcourse your question should be, what is Hyper-V?

Hyper-V is a virtualization platform developed by Microsoft built into Windows Server and Windows. 
It provides hardware virtualization capabilities that enable organizations to create, manage, and run virtual machines at scale. 

It's a Type 1 hypervisor, meaning it runs directly on the computing hardware (bare metal), not on top of an OS and delivering near-native performance 
and robust isolation for virtualized workloads.

> And the VM guest data: This data relates to the operating system and applications running inside the virtual machine.

# The difference between VM host and VM gust data is:

Host-level data gives you an understanding of the VM's overall performance and load, while the guest-level data gives you visibility into the applications, components,
and processes running on the virtual machine and their performance and health.
 
For example, if you’re troubleshooting a performance issue, you might start with host metrics to see which VM is under heavy load,
and then use guest metrics to drill down into the details of the operating system and application performance.
 
Note: One important thing: By default, VM host data is available without additional setup.

However, VM guest data is not available by default, we have to do some additional steps. which we will see after some time.

# VM guest data lets you analyze and troubleshoot the performance and operational efficiency of workloads running on your VMs. 
To monitor VM guest data, you need to install Azure Monitor Agent on the VM and set up a data collection rule (DCR).

